{
  "id": "proj-greenhouse-from-old-windows",
  "slug": "greenhouse-from-old-windows",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Greenhouse from Old Windows",
  "summary": "A walk-in greenhouse framed around salvaged windows. Every one is different; the frame adapts.",
  "difficulty": "advanced",
  "estimatedHours": 40,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Collect the windows first and lay them out on the ground as four walls of about 8 x 10 ft. Test every painted frame with a lead test swab, set aside any with cracked glass, and note each window's size: the frame is drawn from the windows, not the other way round.",
    "Level the site, set concrete deck blocks at the corners and every 4 ft along the long sides, and lay pressure-treated 4x4 sills on them. Screw the corners and check the base is square by measuring equal diagonals.",
    "Frame each wall from 2x4s with studs spaced to each window's width plus 1/4 in and a top plate at one common height. Stand the walls, brace them plumb, and screw them to the sills and to each other at the corners.",
    "Screw the windows into their openings through their own frames, keeping one or two high windows on hinges as vents. Fill odd gaps with offcut boards and run clear silicone wherever a window meets the framing.",
    "Build a gable roof of 2x4 rafters at 24 in centres on a 2x6 ridge, with 2x4 purlins across the rafters. Fix the corrugated polycarbonate on closure strips with washered roofing screws in oversize pilot holes so the panels can expand.",
    "Frame a door from 2x4s skinned with a polycarbonate offcut and hang it on exterior hinges with a gate latch. Fit automatic vent openers to the hinged vents, then prime and paint all exposed wood, including the window frames."
  ],
